\section*{Exact quantum factoring over one fixed finite gate set}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Uniform polynomial-size circuits over one fixed finite bounded-arity gate set are claimed to output every integer factorization with probability one.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Order trials are arranged to have prescribed success probabilities while preserving exact classical records. Circuit phase functions and fraction-label recovery replace arbitrary-angle approximations, and exact amplification plus recursive arithmetic gives a worst-case polynomial factorization family.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Uniform polynomial-size circuits over one fixed finite bounded-arity gate set are claimed to output every integer factorization with probability one. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 279, the selected theorem gives exact probability-one factorization by a polynomially uniform circuit family over a fixed finite bounded-arity gate set. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{ExactQuantumFactoring.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

